CAT = "ukcareDuty"; SEG = "DUT"
# Standard 3 Duty of care, Standard 4 Equality, diversity, inclusion and human rights
Q = [
("What does a duty of care mean for a care worker?",
 "Acting in people's best interests", ["Doing whatever people ask without question", "Keeping people indoors so they stay safe", "Following family wishes over the person's"],
 "A duty of care is a legal obligation to act in the best interests of the people you support and not to act, or fail to act, in a way that causes harm."),
("What is the duty of candour?",
 "Being open when things go wrong", ["Keeping errors within the staff team", "Reporting colleagues to the police", "Sharing records with anyone who asks"],
 "Providers registered with CQC must be open and transparent with people when something goes wrong with their care, give an apology and explain what happened. Workers support this by reporting errors honestly."),
("A person with capacity wants to walk to the shop alone, but you think they might fall. What best balances their rights and your duty of care?",
 "Talk through the risks with them", ["Lock the door until a carer is free", "Refuse and record that they refused care", "Let them go without saying anything"],
 "Duty of care does not mean removing all risk. Talk through the risks, agree safer options with the person and record and report the decision as your agreed ways of working say."),
("A person makes a complaint to you about their care. What should you do first?",
 "Listen and follow the procedure", ["Tell them to write to CQC instead", "Explain that it is not your fault", "Ignore it if it seems minor to you"],
 "Listen without becoming defensive, reassure the person that complaints are welcome and follow your employer's complaints procedure so it is recorded and dealt with."),
("What is a near miss?",
 "An event that could have caused harm", ["An incident that caused serious harm", "A task finished just before its deadline", "A complaint that was later withdrawn"],
 "A near miss is an unplanned event that did not cause harm but could have. Reporting near misses helps prevent the same thing happening again."),
("You realise you gave someone the wrong drink thickness. They seem fine. What should you do?",
 "Report and record it at once", ["Say nothing as no harm was done", "Tell only the person's family", "Wait to see if symptoms appear"],
 "Errors must be reported promptly even when no harm is seen, so the person can be checked and lessons learnt. Hiding mistakes breaks your duty of care and the duty of candour."),
("A person's relative shouts at you in the corridor. What is the best response?",
 "Stay calm, listen, get support", ["Shout back to stand your ground", "Walk away without a word", "Threaten to call the police"],
 "Keep calm, listen to the concern, avoid arguing and get support from a senior colleague if the situation does not settle. Your own safety matters too."),
("Who can a person go to if they are unhappy with how a council-funded or self-funded care service handled their complaint in England?",
 "The Local Government and Social Care Ombudsman", ["The Nursing and Midwifery Council", "The Health and Safety Executive", "The Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman"],
 "When a complaint about adult social care is not resolved by the provider, people can take it to the Local Government and Social Care Ombudsman. CQC wants to hear about concerns but does not resolve individual complaints."),
("What does an accident or incident report need to be?",
 "Factual, accurate and prompt", ["Short, so it is quick to read", "Written by the person harmed", "Kept by you, not the employer"],
 "Reports should state facts, not opinions, be accurate and be completed as soon as possible while details are fresh, following agreed ways of working."),
("What is the main purpose of a risk assessment in a person's care plan?",
 "To enable choices while reducing harm", ["To stop every risky activity safely", "To protect staff from any complaints", "To decide who pays for the care"],
 "Risk assessment helps people do what matters to them as safely as possible. It is a tool for enabling choice, not for removing it."),
("Which law in Great Britain protects people from discrimination because of protected characteristics?",
 "The Equality Act 2010", ["The Care Act 2014", "The Mental Capacity Act 2005", "The Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974"],
 "The Equality Act 2010 brings together discrimination law and lists nine protected characteristics. The Care Act deals with care and support and safeguarding."),
("How many protected characteristics does the Equality Act 2010 list?",
 "Nine", ["Five", "Seven", "Twelve"],
 "They are age, disability, gender reassignment, marriage and civil partnership, pregnancy and maternity, race, religion or belief, sex and sexual orientation."),
("Which of these is a protected characteristic under the Equality Act 2010?",
 "Religion or belief", ["Natural hair colour", "Political party", "Home town or region"],
 "Religion or belief is one of the nine protected characteristics. The others include age, disability, race, sex and sexual orientation."),
("What does inclusion mean in a care setting?",
 "Everyone can take part and belong", ["Everyone gets exactly the same care", "Everyone joins the same activities", "Everyone follows the same routine"],
 "Inclusion means removing barriers so each person can take part fully and feel they belong. It does not mean treating everyone identically."),
("What does treating people equally but not the same mean?",
 "Meeting each person's needs", ["Giving every person the same meal", "Using the same words with everyone", "Ignoring differences between people"],
 "Equality is about fair outcomes. People have different needs and backgrounds, so fair care means adapting support to each person."),
("A colleague makes a joke about a resident's religion. What should you do?",
 "Challenge it or report it", ["Laugh so the team gets on", "Ignore it if the resident is out", "Tell the resident about it"],
 "Discriminatory comments must not go unchallenged. Challenge them if it is safe and appropriate, and report them following your agreed ways of working."),
("Which law in the UK brings the European Convention on Human Rights into domestic law?",
 "The Human Rights Act 1998", ["The Equality Act 2010", "The Care Act 2014", "The Data Protection Act 2018"],
 "The Human Rights Act 1998 lets people rely on Convention rights in UK courts and requires public bodies, and in many cases care providers whose care is arranged or paid for by a council, to respect those rights."),
("Which human right is most at stake when a person is not allowed to choose their own clothes?",
 "Respect for private and family life", ["The right to a fair trial", "Freedom of assembly and association", "The right to free elections"],
 "Article 8 of the Human Rights Act protects private and family life, which includes personal choices about appearance and daily routine."),
("What is unconscious bias?",
 "Judgements made without realising", ["Prejudice a person shows on purpose", "A rule that applies to everyone", "Treating a person more kindly"],
 "Unconscious bias is an attitude or stereotype that affects how we act without us being aware of it. Reflecting on our reactions helps reduce it."),
("A policy says all residents must have a bath, not a shower. Whom could this disadvantage?",
 "People whose faith needs running water", ["People who prefer to eat in the evening", "People who like morning activities", "People who enjoy gardening outdoors"],
 "A rule applied to everyone can put some groups at a disadvantage, for example people whose religion requires washing in running water. This can be indirect discrimination."),
("How can you provide culturally appropriate care?",
 "Ask the person about their customs", ["Assume customs from their country of birth", "Follow your own family's customs", "Treat every person in the same way"],
 "Ask the person, and those close to them if they agree, about their culture, faith, diet and preferences. Assumptions based on background can be wrong."),
("What is direct discrimination?",
 "Treating someone worse for who they are", ["A rule that affects one group more", "Feeling uncomfortable with a person", "Giving extra help to a disabled person"],
 "Direct discrimination is treating a person less favourably because of a protected characteristic. A rule that puts one group at a disadvantage is indirect discrimination."),
("Where can a care worker find reliable information about equality and human rights?",
 "Employer policies and official bodies", ["Comment threads on social media", "Opinions from friends and family", "Any website that appears first"],
 "Agreed ways of working, managers and official bodies such as the Equality and Human Rights Commission give reliable information and support."),
("What should you do if you see a colleague refusing to support a resident because of their sexual orientation?",
 "Report it to your manager", ["Leave it, as it is their view", "Swap residents to avoid conflict", "Raise it only if it happens again"],
 "Refusing care because of sexual orientation is discrimination. Report it through agreed ways of working so the person is protected and the behaviour is addressed."),
]
